          "text": "Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern that helps decouple components by providing their dependencies from the outside rather than having them construct or find those dependencies themselves.\n\nCore idea\n- A \"dependency\" is any object a class needs to do its work (e.g., a repository, service, logger).\n- Instead of a class creating or locating its dependencies, the dependencies are \"injected\" into the class (passed in), typically via the constructor, a setter method, or an interface.\n- This is an application of Inversion of Control (IoC): the class relinquishes control over how dependencies are obtained.\n\nWhy use DI\n- Testability: you can pass in mock or fake dependencies in unit tests.\n- Modularity: components depend on abstractions (interfaces) rather than concrete implementations.\n- Flexibility: swapping implementations (e.g., different data sources) is easy.\n- Single responsibility: classes focus on behavior, not wiring.\n\nSimple example (without DI)\n- class Car creates its own Engine: Car() { engine = new GasEngine(); }\n  - Hard to replace Engine for tests or alternate implementations.\n\nWith DI (constructor injection)\n- class Car { Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; } }\n  - Engine is provided from outside (caller, factory, or DI container).\n\nTypes of injection\n- Constructor injection: dependencies passed through the constructor (preferred for mandatory dependencies).\n- Setter (or property) injection: dependencies set via setter methods (optional or changeable dependencies).\n- Interface injection: the dependency provides an injector method defined by an interface (rare in practice).\n\nDI containers / frameworks\n- Frameworks (Spring, Guice, Dagger, .NET Core DI) can automatically construct object graphs and inject dependencies based on configuration or annotations.\n- Containers are useful for large apps, but introduce runtime configuration and a learning curve.\n\nDI vs Service Locator\n- Service Locator pattern pulls dependencies from a central registry inside the class (the class asks for them).\n- DI favors having dependencies provided to the class, which keeps classes unaware of the container and easier to test.\n\nRelation to SOLID\n- DI supports the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): depend on abstractions, not concretions.\n\nDrawbacks / cautions\n- Overuse or excessive abstraction can make code harder to understand.\n- DI containers add complexity and can hide where objects come from.\n- Improper configuration can cause runtime errors.\n\nBest practices\n- Prefer constructor injection for required dependencies.\n- Use interfaces (or abstract types) to depend on abstractions.\n- Keep wiring code separate from business logic (composition root).\n- Inject small, focused dependencies\u2014avoid large \"god\" services.\n\nIf you want, I can show a short concrete example in Java, C#, or JavaScript (with and without a DI container). Which language would you like?"
